0	section	Talking to the Crew Registrar
1	line	Crew Registrar	Hello there!
1	line	Player	Who are you?
1	line	Crew Registrar	I'm the crew registrar. Here to cut through the red tape of crew management for anyone who needs it!
1	line	Player	A lot of paperwork in managing a crew is there?
1	line	Crew Registrar	You'd be surprised. Quality assurance guideline QA-275 states all crew must be appropriately fit and healthy after being subjected to QA form QA-275.
1	line	Crew Registrar	QA-123 requires all crew to pass a mandatory cargo data protection course if the vessel can be fitted with a cargo hold.
1	line	Crew Registrar	Then of course we have QA-441, which defines the background checks that any crewmate operating a sail must pass. And all that's without even considering QA- 215...
1	line	Player	That all sounds a bit much!
1	line	Crew Registrar	Well, it's not for everyone. Still, if you ever find yourself in need of a crew, you know where to find me.
1	action	end
0	section	Talking to Ribs
1	line	Ribs	Ahoy, young whippersnapper. Have you come to hear a tale from old Ribs?
1	line	Player	Kind of, I guess. I was hoping you could tell me about Will and Anne, along with the map they stole.
1	line	Ribs	A dark day. A very dark day. Those young fools know not what they have wrought upon themselves.
1	line	Player	That being?
1	line	Ribs	A curse! A curse most foul! That map marked the resting place of the Crimson Kraken! She was a deadly vessel, captained by none other than Barnacle Barnaby himself!
1	line	Player	Barnacle Barnaby? Who's that then?
1	line	Ribs	You have not heard the tale of Barnacle Barnaby? You have not heard of the scourge of the Unquiet Ocean?
1	select	Select an option
1	option	No, tell me about him.
2	line	Player	No, tell me about him.
2	line	Ribs	He was a cruel and vicious pirate. Orchestrator of the Driftwood Massacre and unleasher of the Plague of Black Mists. He believed himself untouchable, but his arrogance was his undoing.
2	line	Ribs	Despite the warnings of many, Barnacle Barnaby attempted to plunder the Skullhorde, but his only reward was a deadly curse. It destroyed the Crimson Kraken, condemning all on board to a watery grave.
2	line	Player	And you had a map leading to the wreck of the Kraken?
2	line	Ribs	Aye! I purchased the map from Ulrich the Unfortunate, the sole survivor of the Kraken, but I had no intention of using it. I knew that no good would ever come from seeking the Kraken.
2	line	Player	Then why not destroy the map?
2	line	Ribs	And risk the wrath of Barnaby's ghost? No. Better the map be locked away, so that neither it nor the curse could harm anyone else.
2	line	Player	I'm not sure I quite follow your reasoning for keeping it, but I guess it doesn't matter now. I'm afraid Will and Anne found and looted the Kraken.
2	action	continues
1	option	Now's not really the time for tales.
2	line	Player	Now's not really the time for tales.
2	line	Ribs	You are a fool for thinking that way, but on your head be it. The short of it is that Barnaby and the Kraken were cursed, condemning him and his crew to a watery grave.
2	line	Player	I see. Well, I'm afraid Will and Anne found and looted the Kraken.
2	action	continues
1	line	Ribs	I feared as much, though part of me still hoped it would not come to be. Tell me, did they find Barnaby's medallion?
1	line	Player	They did. It was actually the only thing they were interested in.
1	line	Ribs	Then the foul curse is now free once more. They must be stopped!
1	line	Player	Well, I do have my own personal reasons for wanting Will and Anne dealt with. Maybe I could try and find them.
1	line	Ribs	You are a brave soul indeed, but that is just what is needed at a time like this.
1	line	Ribs	You should talk to Steve. He already put out a bounty for Will and Anne after the theft. He might have heard something about where they could have gone.
1	action	end
0	section	Talking to Ribs again
